  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of what the manifests establish:\n\n**Real edge: babel \u2192 next.js.** `packages/next/package.json` lists `@babel/core`, `@babel/traverse`, `@babel/generator`, `@babel/types`, `@babel/parser` (transitively via `@babel/core`), and multiple `@babel/plugin-*`/`@babel/preset-*` packages as `devDependencies`. These aren't just build-time tooling \u2014 `packages/next/src/bundles/babel/bundle.js` re-exports them for runtime use, and `packages/next/src/build/babel/loader/transform.ts` + `get-config.ts` reimplement Babel's file-transform pipeline (`normalizeFile`, `traverse`, `generate`) directly against Babel's internals, feeding a set of custom plugins (`next-ssg-transform.ts`, `commonjs.ts`, `next-page-config.ts`, etc.) that pattern-match specific AST node shapes. This is the repo genuinely reached by a new `DecoratorMetadata` node \u2014 both because it walks arbitrary user ASTs and because it hand-rolls logic normally owned by `@babel/core`.\n\n**No real edge: babel \u2192 webpack.** Webpack's root `package.json` has `@babel/core` and `@babel/preset-react` only under `devDependencies`, used with `babel-loader` for webpack's own test fixtures/examples. Webpack's actual bundling engine parses source with `acorn`/`acorn-import-assertions` (listed in `dependencies`), never `@babel/parser`, and I found no `@babel/*` import anywhere under `lib/`. Webpack is the \"expected but not reached\" case.\n\n**Orthogonal edge: next.js \u2192 webpack.** `packages/next/package.json` also pins `webpack: 5.86.0` as a devDependency (vendored into `next/dist/compiled/webpack`), but that's a bundling-pipeline dependency, not an AST-consumption one \u2014 irrelevant to this specific change.",
